{
 "idea": {
  "id": "div_runup",
  "family": "calendar",
  "title": "Own regular dividend payers into the ex-dividend date; short them after it",
  "why": "Hartzmark and Solomon (2013) find dividend payers earn abnormal returns in the days before the ex-dividend date and on it, and give some back in the weeks after: buyers who want the payment (income funds, retail, holders reinvesting) arrive before the date and nobody is paid to stand in their way for so small a move. The ex-day itself has long shown a price drop smaller than the dividend (Elton and Gruber 1970), because the marginal holder is taxed on it. The date and amount are declared weeks ahead, so this is a calendar fact, like a report date. The losing side is the income buyer who pays up before the date and the holder who sells after it. The test: is the run-up there in 2010-2021 in liquid names, is it in the days before (`pre3`), on the day (`exday`) or both (`thru3`, `thru5`); does it grow with the size of the payment (`thru3_high_yield`), as demand for the dividend should; and is there a fall afterwards (`post3_short`, `pre_post_ls`). Ex-dates and amounts are read from the price history itself: the gap between the total-return close and the split-only close steps on each ex-date by the dividend over the prior close. Only payers with a payment about a quarter earlier are used, so the date was predictable and one-off specials are left out.",
  "source": "Hartzmark and Solomon 2013 (The dividend month premium); Elton and Gruber 1970",
  "market": "us",
  "universe": {
   "min_price": 5,
   "min_adv": 20000000.0
  },
  "base": {
   "side": "long",
   "select": "sign",
   "hedge": true,
   "hold": 3,
   "entry": "close",
   "max_w": 0.05,
   "lead": 3,
   "min_yield": 0.0
  },
